#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198082 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2aee319-68d6-54f7-8023-b24d32610bc4" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching Cartesian Products of Special Sets in the Coordinate Plane"}] :question/id #uuid "eae94514-cd92-5405-90b1-ee09dad18e13" :question/math-academy-id "e-13162" :question/problem "Sketch a representation of the set $S = \\mathbb{Z} \\times \\mathbb{N}$ in the Cartesian plane." :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A \\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B = \\big\\{(a,b) \\::\\: a\\in A,\\, b\\in B\\big\\}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS = \\mathbb{Z} \\times \\mathbb{N}\n$$\n\ncontains every point in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ whose $x$-coordinate is an integer number and whose $y$-coordinate is a natural number.\n\n$$\nS=\n\\Big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: x\\in \\mathbb Z,\\, y\\in \\mathbb N\\Big\\}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider $S_1\\subset S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate is any integer number and whose $y$-coordinate equals ${\\color{red}{1}}.$\n\n$$\nS_1 = \\Big\\{ (x, {\\color{red}{1}}) \\: : \\: x\\in \\mathbb Z\\Big\\}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent $S_1$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/6b63e973bbcc3e25d601a1f6cef97f5a00cc22e738c46d04a1ac06851421894e.png)\n\nNext, consider $S_2\\subset S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate is any integer number and whose $y$-coordinate equals ${\\color{blue}{2}}.$\n\n$$\nS_2 = \\Big\\{ (x,{\\color{blue}{2}}) \\: : \\: x\\in \\mathbb Z\\Big\\}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent $S_2$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/f24ce8f7610d096c795a8a74b0b714b720c36442042c28b66432d6b2102fb9d4.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets $S_3,S_4,S_5,\\ldots,$ similarly. Our Cartesian product $S$ is the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={S}_{1}\\cup {S}_{2}\\cup {S}_{3}\\cup {S}_{4}\\cup … \\\\  & ={⋃}_{i\\in N}{S}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize $\\mathbb{Z} \\times \\mathbb{N}$ as the set of points in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/c999c61d8ffbb27d47272811e32340cef3253b4730e684be10615487a8b27200.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198097 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3a2b291-c6dd-58c8-a10f-d851511d7bfc" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching Cartesian Products in the Coordinate Plane"}] :question/id #uuid "bb40c174-3739-50df-835e-5d47c9902d5b" :question/math-academy-id "e-13192" :question/problem "Sketch a representation of the set $S = \\{1, 2\\} \\times (0, 1)$ in the Cartesian plane." :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A \\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B = \\big\\{(a,b) \\::\\: a\\in A,\\, b\\in B\\big\\}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS = \\{1,2\\} \\times (0,1)\n$$\n\ncontains every point in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ whose $x$-coordinate is either $1$ or $2$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $(0,1).$\n\n$$\nS=\n\\Big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: x\\in \\{{\\color{red}{1}},{\\color{blue}{2}}\\},\\, y\\in (0,1) \\Big\\}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider $S_1\\subset S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals ${\\color{red}{1}}$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $(0,1).$\n\n$$\nS_1 = \\Big\\{ ({\\color{red}{1}},y) \\: : \\: y \\in(0,1) \\Big\\}\n$$\n\nThis set is represented by the line segment shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/56f200fce557749a56b9b5b51bc670da5f8c7f2cdb0196392ee80efcd6be980a.png)\n\nNext, consider $S_2\\subset S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals ${\\color{blue}{2}}$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $(0,1).$\n\n$$\nS_2 = \\big\\{ ({\\color{blue}{2}},y) \\: : \\: y \\in(0,1) \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThis set is represented by the line segment shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/f96074cc265bba6eb59717364d2aa5390fdccea4d398fe5ae0b99f7ad82f0b93.png)\n\nOur Cartesian product is the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\nS = S_1\\cup S_2\n$$\n\nTherefore, $S$ can be represented by the two line segments shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/0c0529aaa680b4b572d17f2cfbfb76d805134e72c50ab3ed469286cc025185a3.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198133 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6290e701-b4ea-5a86-ab7e-7371c5bdc11e" :knowledge-point/title "Visualizing Unions of Uncountable Families of Indexed Sets"}] :question/id #uuid "aa337681-5d8c-5ea1-b0fe-5d8cd15040a7" :question/math-academy-id "e-13320" :question/problem "Sketch a representation of the set $\\,\\displaystyle \\bigcup_{i \\in I} X_i$ in the Cartesian plane given that $I = [-1,1] \\subset \\mathbb{R}$ and $X_i = \\{ i \\} \\times [0, \\, i^2].$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the union of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *at least one* set:\n\n$$\n\\bigcup_{i \\in I} A_i = \\big\\{ x \\: : \\: x \\in A_k \\:\\:\\textrm{for at least one}\\:\\: k \\in I \\big\\}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=-0.5,$ we have \n\n$$\nX_{-0.5} = \\{ -0.5 \\} \\times [0, 0.25] = \\big\\{ (-0.5,k) \\: : \\: k \\in [0, 0.25] \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a vertical line segment from the point $(-0.5, 0)$ to the point $(-0.5,0.25)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/ccc75eac899efcab1d0b6fde12609a3ea8f61d6fec74c739ef3393fad32645a9.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\nX_i = \\{ i \\} \\times [0, i^2] = \\big\\{ (i,k) \\: : \\: k \\in [0, i^2] \\big\\}\n$$\n\nis the vertical line segment from the point $(i,0)$ to the point $(i, i^2).$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/60c98be5d97748e9285bb5e9c30bba9781a5ef99245789718a7828b18a6df8fb.png)\n\n \n\nAs $i$ runs over $I,$ these line segments sweep the following figure that represents the union $\\displaystyle \\bigcup_{i \\in I} X_i{:}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/b3a61d686c78c76a4f461c1c771437d31030339b634661da8b8dc4f492db55eb.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198134 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5793f07d-e0de-5707-a5e1-32d657244e0b" :knowledge-point/title "Visualizing Intersections of Uncountable Families of Indexed Sets"}] :question/id #uuid "67569060-2f33-5a8d-ae73-690742d3d318" :question/math-academy-id "e-13321" :question/problem "Let $I = [0,1] \\subset \\mathbb{R}$ and $X_i = [-i, 0] \\times [-1,1].$ Sketch a representation of $\\displaystyle \\bigcap_{i \\in I} X_i$ in the Cartesian plane." :question/worked-solution "Recall that the intersection of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *all* the sets:\n\n$$\n\\bigcap_{i \\in I} A_i = \\big\\{ x \\: : \\: x \\in A_k \\:\\:\\textrm{for all}\\:\\: k \\in I \\big\\}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=0.5,$ we have \n\n$$\nX_{0.5} = [-0.5,0] \\times [-1, 1] = \\big\\{ (a,b) \\: : \\: a \\in [-0.5, 0], b \\in [-1, 1] \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a rectangle whose vertices are at $(-0.5, -1),$ $(-0.5, 1),$ $(0, 1),$ and $(0, -1)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/8ad650cb3f2fa4da920a442e8cc1b24af5027595cba4e43138eff2126cf4c102.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\nX_i = [-i, 0] \\times [-1,1] = \\big\\{ (a,b) \\: : \\: a \\in [-i, 0], b \\in [-1, 1] \\big\\}\n$$\n\nis the rectangle whose vertices are at $(-i,-1),$ $(-i,1),$ $(0, 1),$ and $(0, -1).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/a37cfa508cf3a60536084fbc1e57c5aeac3414904d2ad72ef1deda85dc4eddd8.png)\n\n \n\nThe intersection of all these rectangles is the line segment $\\{0\\}\\times [-1,1]{:}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/5e9a611629f4370c57e77a057439c759670afa17c14c4b2339e1d471f616ea72.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198644 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5793f07d-e0de-5707-a5e1-32d657244e0b" :knowledge-point/title "Visualizing Intersections of Uncountable Families of Indexed Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "132f77bd-9ddf-5caf-96a1-e220936f45d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$∅,$ the empty set" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307934} {:answer/id #uuid "098babed-40a5-5df9-8de1-0a88c19800f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/df4a1ecffe6302ee57250944ec8f178f46a066b2edff2ed1afb200e7b0217cf7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307935} {:answer/id #uuid "715d6dad-5b8d-5a07-ab6f-cc9c2db51162" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/6b5b39546eabacdf948bd87e793727fdc8cd8f2664df037be53bfb630fc41670.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307936} {:answer/id #uuid "280a367a-db86-510d-83e6-8f1354018cdd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/506d86848903de9f038d541152aabf67d99d0f4d0a7e5f5772b811ff1cf9dade.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307937} {:answer/id #uuid "51ec42ac-80bd-51b7-9c6b-1a00dd8a1002" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/17a176a0e7cb254483d93741cd2c1e3db581005f479928ac36809e55bc970c07.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307938}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "132f77bd-9ddf-5caf-96a1-e220936f45d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$∅,$ the empty set" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307934} :answer-field/id #uuid "dda8c1e1-bad9-5b1c-b928-a0d2962fffd5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307933}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "30b7b0c8-79fe-53ad-bb30-601d2f7e078d" :question/math-academy-id "q-172690" :question/problem "Let $I=[0,2]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}={i}\\times [0,i].$ Which of the following represents ${⋂}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the intersection of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *all* the sets:\n\n$$\n{⋂}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}={x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for all}\\,\\,k\\in I}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=1,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}={1}\\times [0,1]={(1,k)\\,:\\,k\\in [0,1]}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a vertical line segment from the point $(1,0)$ to the point $(1,1)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/71dd97607df48caf579c2e476520507ede035f65c336064f022cd900b6c120fc.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}={i}\\times [0,i]={(i,k)\\,:\\,k\\in [0,i]}\n$$\n\nis the vertical line segment from the point $(i,0)$ to the point $(i,i).$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/4b87c830620f54e5b3a3bb7923be971b4d9d4abb98cfb3dcdc8387f376a0b452.png)\n\nThere are no points that lie in all sets. Therefore, the intersection of the indexed sets is $∅,$ the empty set."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307939 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5793f07d-e0de-5707-a5e1-32d657244e0b" :knowledge-point/title "Visualizing Intersections of Uncountable Families of Indexed Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e6e36dc9-ca9d-5871-ac65-5a8e2fd56e8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/a2786a8cfb41d8fb352c95dc36688fb2eff4773680be54be9dd3d1c696ae588a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307941} {:answer/id #uuid "dfb187e0-61a4-5b94-8c71-8517589c46d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$∅,$ the empty set" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307942} {:answer/id #uuid "46ae9087-5cf5-516b-81da-46b3beae2ed4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/4fa495b2b7e5528f5aa70abe8ce778fc0c1e7d5bcd4a9b855a72ee1469ed5f01.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307943} {:answer/id #uuid "3452be50-9b3a-545e-9265-22146b078969" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/3b3d7d98bdc1c336c9151bff06f7d07bea7e2228efb253b0fc16cdd2de1faa99.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307944} {:answer/id #uuid "1e664514-cba7-564e-8f55-c3cbd248245f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/e5ed1251cb899e92d9f2e7dd6111140b07556432b5240fa5a9efacd33ce19989.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307945}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dfb187e0-61a4-5b94-8c71-8517589c46d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$∅,$ the empty set" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307942} :answer-field/id #uuid "dead5edd-27f4-5ebd-8ce8-6749e3e0cbe8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307940}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "280b10a4-5dc0-5bb2-b73b-6d6eeefafeca" :question/math-academy-id "q-175295" :question/problem "Let $I=[0,2]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}=[0,i]\\times {-i}.$ Which of the following represents ${⋂}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the intersection of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *all* the sets:\n\n$$\n{⋂}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}={x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for all}\\,\\,k\\in I}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=1,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}=[0,1]\\times {-1}={(k,-1)\\,:\\,k\\in [0,1]}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a horizontal line segment from the point $(0,-1)$ to the point $(1,-1)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/a39443fd1b3764ff60c0c052085134ad7eeda6759c07c4d936fc03cd121f72dd.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=[0,i]\\times {-i}={(k,-i)\\,:\\,k\\in [0,i]}\n$$\n\nis the horizontal line segment from the point $(0,-i)$ to the point $(i,-i).$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/7f18af14c55acc4bc2cd63b601dba0bde3cd51c0284b398b401c854537003926.png)\n\nThere are no points that lie in all sets. Therefore, the intersection of the indexed sets is $∅,$ the empty set."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377568 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5793f07d-e0de-5707-a5e1-32d657244e0b" :knowledge-point/title "Visualizing Intersections of Uncountable Families of Indexed Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13ff5d1a-feec-53e4-82e7-fe941b1e17db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/0b689347086db4bf406a17c633b83dbc924d4c4963a64a2ca0efa7321bd6e1b3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377570} {:answer/id #uuid "b09a62ba-cd7e-56b8-bd63-b79d35418963" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$∅,$ the empty set" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377571} {:answer/id #uuid "c2286fa1-8de3-519f-b51e-507acf6dae24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/f74ac291edafc3808c7bb195f5295f50f5bac7a7d9db45310f66df95288e92cd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377572} {:answer/id #uuid "3709c48b-5ca9-5b53-888f-2969ecd99084" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/c8024f88915b3d2d4cc83a75e95df538973d574703faf2cc16ee4ea31f82c074.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377573} {:answer/id #uuid "52b7e911-e344-5509-83e0-a69486c65424" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/23970d1fbb8aaf33240272aa47741d675d114969fff75576f28413f8668b76b9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377574}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b09a62ba-cd7e-56b8-bd63-b79d35418963" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$∅,$ the empty set" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377571} :answer-field/id #uuid "19620cc4-dede-5351-bfa3-99df56059f65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377569}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5ece46e8-cec5-5bd2-9703-25094685d96d" :question/math-academy-id "q-175303" :question/problem "Let $I=[0,1]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}={i}\\times [0,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi i)].$ Which of the following represents ${⋂}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the intersection of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *all* the sets:\n\n$$\n{⋂}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}={x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for all}\\,\\,k\\in I}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=\\frac{1}{2},$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{1/2}={\\frac{1}{2}}\\times [0,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})]={\\frac{1}{2}}\\times [0,1]={(\\frac{1}{2},k)\\,:\\,k\\in [0,1]}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a vertical line segment from the point $(\\frac{1}{2},0)$ to the point $(\\frac{1}{2},1)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/2827c5b33e84aff775d8bcc7ca98492afb220dda3a52fec4166afaa4da187d66.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}={i}\\times [0,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi i)]={(i,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi k))\\,:\\,k\\in [0,i]}\n$$\n\nis the vertical line segment from the point $(i,0)$ to the point $(i,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi i)).$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/5a108a3c0cdf37a072d135674b6ffdb3894b8c5ba142604ea7a434b0f42245f5.png)\n\nThere are no points that lie in all sets. Therefore, the intersection of the indexed sets is $∅,$ the empty set."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377575 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6290e701-b4ea-5a86-ab7e-7371c5bdc11e" :knowledge-point/title "Visualizing Unions of Uncountable Families of Indexed Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a96e779-3fca-5b20-8cd9-41d8b94851e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/db4bea7c2f08abb66318443c88a0504bbfdc3d9597b3f42a8808795ebc78b172.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377577} {:answer/id 