 of the following represents ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the union of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *at least one* set:\n\n$$\n{⋃}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}={x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for at least one}\\,\\,k\\in I}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=0.5,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{0.5}=[-0.5,0.5]\\times {1}={(k,1)\\,:\\,k\\in [-0.5,0.5]}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a horizontal line segment from the point $(-0.5,1)$ to the point $(0.5,1)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/6c6aafd9ea78868e953340eaab3862491d275972d690e2ceb27c213dadbcaea6.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=[-i,i]\\times {2i}={(k,2i)\\,:\\,k\\in [-i,i]}\n$$\n\nis the horizontal line segment from the point $(-i,2i)$ to the point $(i,2i).$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/02c8f93a25d7b510d3e238e6d4a974d6ccc2e83f3ac01fe92ce4535fbb4dce48.png)\n\nAs $i$ runs over $I,$ these line segments sweep the following triangle that represents the union ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/3b3cefa03d71d99e4b7f3b6852ed36512d2a841a37c558034eac5c4b6870799c.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307953 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6290e701-b4ea-5a86-ab7e-7371c5bdc11e" :knowledge-point/title "Visualizing Unions of Uncountable Families of Indexed Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd15d26e-bdf3-5fe0-84b9-980b25f01709" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/8ce0c60aafe1a41b372c1f1477241c584d6f8ae224113c360576feb37fc25a8c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307955} {:answer/id #uuid "129832fc-dc2b-57ac-840e-63de2e90981a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/fcda8aaa1da0ee1e9fee4957246497a927b4373c51d8a46db429540ea51f5083.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307956} {:answer/id #uuid "1231c5af-cdb2-54d0-952b-de9c2a8060ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/cd35c57fa9c6ce3ae2ee5961b8ae3e7d16ea1a2aa44c302a9e40f19b6c1b11ea.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307957} {:answer/id #uuid "f6fe220a-a262-58d6-ac9b-4071188d639e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/b6518f0f49f1147742d621fa940d49f84e7cce9c17d071fdfeb4a2e96abbf591.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307958} {:answer/id #uuid "d217b59c-798f-51ff-abfb-5b1969eb6aa4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/310ba014b846afb8d4f2bff610e762da71b47e8c4daa2d6ffebf4b62ecc4d1db.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307959}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd15d26e-bdf3-5fe0-84b9-980b25f01709" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/8ce0c60aafe1a41b372c1f1477241c584d6f8ae224113c360576feb37fc25a8c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307955} :answer-field/id #uuid "39d5bd33-5d17-5469-8cbe-cdc0b0c554f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307954}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "15afbaad-5b0e-58d9-8df7-ceeef530333d" :question/math-academy-id "q-177652" :question/problem "Let $I=[-1,1]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}={i}\\times [-|i|,|i|].$ Which of the following represents ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the union of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *at least one* set:\n\n$$\n{⋃}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}={x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for at least one}\\,\\,k\\in I}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=-0.5,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{-0.5}={-0.5}\\times [-0.5,0.5]={(-0.5,k)\\,:\\,k\\in [-0.5,0.5]}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a vertical line segment from the point $(-0.5,-0.5)$ to the point $(-0.5,0.5)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/b7793d85e02ccd05c55c34971c90be087d58274eabd21f46fe09f08ccd60797b.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}={i}\\times [-|i|,|i|]={(i,k)\\,:\\,k\\in [-|i|,|i|]}\n$$\n\nis the vertical line segment from the point $(i,-|i|)$ to the point $(i,|i|).$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/c30712295adfe51c655662cc09f8f1e23b3ea97573693154e0e0b6ba48e15c07.png)\n\nAs $i$ runs over $I,$ these line segments sweep the following figure that represents the union ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/8ce0c60aafe1a41b372c1f1477241c584d6f8ae224113c360576feb37fc25a8c.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198706 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2aee319-68d6-54f7-8023-b24d32610bc4" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching Cartesian Products of Special Sets in the Coordinate Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a6bdfa9f-c500-5b96-8971-2bf1616d470b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4387/Source/Images/q-181304-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195218} {:answer/id #uuid "b74af9cb-b8b3-54fc-a2a8-1fe6ff3fda78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4387/Source/Images/q-181304-a-4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195219} {:answer/id #uuid "c9144558-717c-543a-a3b2-93216e1bcad9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4387/Source/Images/q-181304-a-5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195220} {:answer/id #uuid "1a5eabee-c232-5f0c-aaab-f80920e49ee5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4387/Source/Images/q-181304-a-2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195221} {:answer/id #uuid "30ace25b-f9ea-5ff3-a325-ddeebf8cd467" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4387/Source/Images/q-181304-a-3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195222}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6bdfa9f-c500-5b96-8971-2bf1616d470b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4387/Source/Images/q-181304-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195218} :answer-field/id #uuid "173c08a3-67c6-519b-b556-b229312be8b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195217}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0d77329f-c53c-509c-8856-d83cfdd56ec9" :question/math-academy-id "q-181304" :question/problem "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S=N\\times N$ in the Cartesian plane?" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS=N\\times N\n$$\n\ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is a natural number and whose $y$-coordinate is a natural number.\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in N,\\,y\\in N}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider ${S}_{1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any natural number.\n\n$$\n{S}_{1}={(1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in N}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/34f779801b1bb52d5834ab4e04c91d92585af9876f5ea687ad59cb6931e0144c.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{2}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $2$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any natural number.\n\n$$\n{S}_{2}={(2,y)\\,:\\,y\\in N}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{2}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/387709ae58ce7716338785f82e9159ce8570b046518554edd820c3ed31a23c53.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets ${S}_{3},{S}_{4},{S}_{5},…$ similarly. Our Cartesian product $S$ is the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={S}_{1}\\cup {S}_{2}\\cup {S}_{3}\\cup … \\\\  & ={⋃}_{i\\in N}{S}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize $N\\times N$ as the set of points in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/141d0b84041957cf8415db4673c999f5213ff23553e57ab7e0e9120e648f306c.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377582 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3a2b291-c6dd-58c8-a10f-d851511d7bfc" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching Cartesian Products in the Coordinate Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4776dbc6-8794-5c53-a8cb-80404fd745e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/075454dae9ed36967a70537cdf3a3f59931cdd81671ff4059658d2e0df438898.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377584} {:answer/id #uuid "19536bf3-d230-5da5-8b8a-4713f5008d48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/c28ba6b0da8364f4e7f157b58827384f9e138a2c0fc53558ce7ab2df2f646747.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377585} {:answer/id #uuid "67be282f-bb45-5a62-95a9-0e246e6b2d79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/82f9ecf98b8b2b4e5f75b3130e8483354c93922dc3a942e271562ac1ad17438e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377586} {:answer/id #uuid "dc04add8-f9fb-5bc0-94b6-32dfcedf6172" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/e0fa3e717af02a353db77ae8b357dc338c975166fb5fbe6c5c1309d1c1f511f2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377587} {:answer/id #uuid "4791ff23-185c-55c8-85f2-c7c4c028023c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/2922ff5e3a32e01fbc292898c88c1d8eca8d933897d4b9d3c1694b6f5cbeb2a2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377588}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4776dbc6-8794-5c53-a8cb-80404fd745e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/075454dae9ed36967a70537cdf3a3f59931cdd81671ff4059658d2e0df438898.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377584} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b16d8bf-f59d-50e9-95b1-ca63b493d122" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377583}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "734eb671-4f30-5ac2-952a-94e297467ed9" :question/math-academy-id "q-181306" :question/problem "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S=[-1,1]\\times {-1,1}$ in the Cartesian plane?" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS=[-1,1]\\times {-1,1}\n$$\n\ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[-1,1]$ and whose $y$-coordinate is either $-1$ or $1.$\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in [-1,1],\\,y\\in {-1,1}}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider ${S}_{1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[-1,1]$ and whose $y$-coordinate equals $-1$\n\n$$\n{S}_{1}={(x,-1)\\,:\\,x\\in [-1,1]}\n$$\n\nThis set is represented by the line segment shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/3ed05ffc58aad1a6c75e7862c1175f497fb14870e6650cc6c4ce01c3df091d8b.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{2}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[-1,1]$ and whose $y$-coordinate equals $1$\n\n$$\n{S}_{2}={(x,1)\\,:\\,x\\in [-1,1]}.\n$$\n\nThis set is represented by the line segment shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/0f57920b2532177b0d64ac3dc95feb6a476bb385112d736d702895862e7de2b2.png)\n\nOur Cartesian product is the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\nS={S}_{1}\\cup {S}_{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $S$ can be represented by the two line segments shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/075454dae9ed36967a70537cdf3a3f59931cdd81671ff4059658d2e0df438898.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377589 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6290e701-b4ea-5a86-ab7e-7371c5bdc11e" :knowledge-point/title "Visualizing Unions of Uncountable Families of Indexed Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5242b65-0dd2-5811-a2fd-83d8b6a9fbb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/6bc02e0957ea0099329b8266d4960b542d86c19f44b4fc811ba0752512def666.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377591} {:answer/id #uuid "6a9f2766-9176-565e-9005-86cb985c3513" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/070389fcdb6d69f9aa90dabbd98a0d3461e26d3e3e08b1d53b92e99688c09923.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377592} {:answer/id #uuid "90a904db-08a8-578e-9537-9af867244366" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/0f219c170d7e73a8a5c3d11afabe6763e25ade77be9ed8a0b7495a8d9f6be90c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377593} {:answer/id #uuid "4640f671-87ff-5b8c-be17-90e5c65324c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/4233644fb8c5e19f379fdbc001951869fd5d9062e1f14d0abbb53d677780fb0a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377594} {:answer/id #uuid "4761020c-b561-5f0b-9113-5dde8b234e98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/91f80f30887e5233d12ee814c1e991f81613bd52c9afd7334397b123b68b8bc7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377595}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a9f2766-9176-565e-9005-86cb985c3513" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/070389fcdb6d69f9aa90dabbd98a0d3461e26d3e3e08b1d53b92e99688c09923.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377592} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b6af7b2-85bf-5d28-a4af-4ace88731227" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377590}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "08ecacda-324b-5560-9b50-f8f610c3a69d" :question/math-academy-id "q-181775" :question/problem "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S=R\\times R$ in the Cartesian plane?" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set\n\n$$\nS=R\\times R\n$$\n\ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is any real number and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number.\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in R,\\,y\\in R}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider ${S}_{0}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $0$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number.\n\n$$\n{S}_{0}={(0,y)\\,:\\,y\\in R}\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{0}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/07a3d9a6c38b5bfcc529496735a1a66eaceefd5ae3437a2cb0cd45911eccafae.png)\n\nNow, consider a general ${S}_{i}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals some $i\\in R$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number.\n\n$$\n{S}_{i}={(i,y)\\,:\\,y\\in R}\n$$\n\nFor each $i\\in R,$ ${S}_{i}$ is a vertical line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/e454f1cf125486ca25acf15e26efd251a4a03b08e899814ab3e77db7898713c4.png)\n\nOur Cartesian product $S$ is the union of these subsets over $R:$\n\n$$\nS={⋃}_{i\\in R}{S}_{i}.\n$$\n\nThis is the whole plane, as shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/070389fcdb6d69f9aa90dabbd98a0d3461e26d3e3e08b1d53b92e99688c09923.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307967 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2aee319-68d6-54f7-8023-b24d32610bc4" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching Cartesian Products of Special Sets in the Coordinate Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95fdd016-0bad-5595-b4a6-0749069f92b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/4535a7c6aa7fe8f358d3463b4654c735e53df373d1001916dc035290ca54f969.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307969} {:answer/id #uuid "a0af478e-12ba-5c4d-bde3-b3f6ca1ed4f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/664f327f59e76cb22e1b9bd0a83f84c24e75ad6a0d176532437f629bf80bfc29.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307970} {:answer/id #uuid "b320fcd3-9039-5aa1-b7d8-01e0cf9387c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/89ac9e71d8152097657f0c01daecf2b894dc142db4ba27fbfadcb8ded6e9cb19.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307971} {:answer/id #uuid "b44fbb1b-82be-5d2a-a104-74bf816fc6af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/4ddec65745de14217112f6365d9dd372df5a24a13ef8bedc8db592c9ee18cab1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307972} {:answer/id #uuid "cea7954c-3904-52ec-9cb4-2bf067d4efe2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/05323cccad18bb4664fe74112ff312f53ef3e12016be6f7b30ca9146f2ba6119.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307973}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b320fcd3-9039-5aa1-b7d8-01e0cf9387c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/89ac9e71d8152097657f0c01daecf2b894dc142db4ba27fbfadcb8ded6e9cb19.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307971} :answer-field/id #uuid "542d5677-3b19-503d-ac3a-c335de48d318" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307968}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ef5d7cb6-447b-5fde-822f-0d2b0fd3c5e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-181777" :question/problem "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S=R\\times Z$ in the Cartesian plane?" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS=R\\times Z\n$$\n\n \ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is a real number and whose $y$-coordinate is an integer number.\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in R,\\,y\\in Z}\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S,$ consider ${S}_{-1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate is any real number and whose $y$-coordinate equals $-1.$\n\n$$\n{S}_{-1}={(x,-1)\\,:\\,x\\in R}\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{-1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/8e677c814756c4818c25da450d789c974787df60cf4bb18017a96593d3ecc180.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate is any real number and whose $y$-coordinate equals $1.$\n\n$$\n{S}_{1}={(x,1)\\,:\\,x\\in R}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/f99beeb00c070048ee4ee7c1b6c9e077d7d4e0f3fd9e63aa12b5b58588e14dbb.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets $…{S}_{-3},{S}_{-2},{S}_{0},{S}_{2},{S}_{3},…$ similarly. Our Cartesian product consists of the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\times Z & =…{S}_{-2}\\cup {S}_{-1}\\cup {S}_{0}\\cup {S}_{1}\\cup {S}_{2}\\cup … \\\\  & ={⋃}_{i\\in Z}{S}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize $R\\times Z$ as the set of horizontal lines shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/89ac9e71d8152097657f0c01daecf2b894dc142db4ba27fbfadcb8ded6e9cb19.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198729 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3a2b291-c6dd-58c8-a10f-d851511d7bfc" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching Cartesian Products in the Coordinate Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd1d32a9-cfd5-529f-a9c5-4124d3fff3d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4387/Source/Images/q-181949-a-5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195394} {:answer/id #uuid "f62eb4a7-4e5a-5855-b06e-55459d1747fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4387/Source/Images/q-181949-a-3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195395} {:answer/id #uuid "1ecc40f6-f148-5be7-99e4-0e62de82948f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4387/Source/Images/q-181949-a-2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195396} {:answer/id #uuid "2ff1c24b-4180-5408-854f-65dae85655ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4387/Source/Images/q-181949-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195397} {:answer/id #uuid "30580768-dd97-5527-b3ff-730be3c9de92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4387/Source/Images/q-181949-a-4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195398}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ff1c24b-4180-5408-854f-65dae85655ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4387/Source/Images/q-181949-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195397} :answer-field/id #uuid "129a7ac0-6839-5c07-b5fd-fbb809ec9bf8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195393}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "24f0a183-4542-54dd-8464-0891330af854" :question/math-academy-id "q-181949" :question/problem "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S={0,1,2}\\times {-1,1}$ in the Cartesian plane?" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nIn this case, computing ${0,1,2}\\times {-1,1}$ gives the following set:\n\n$$\nS={(0,-1),(0,1),(1,-1),(1,1),(2,-1),(2,1)}.\n$$\n\nOur visualization of this set is shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/c5fdae9b803df3873ee7deef4a36678e0d8274dd256fe331360261f1fe6dd492.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377596 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2aee319-68d6-54f7-8023-b24d32610bc4" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching Cartesian Products of Special Sets in the Coordinate Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7a3826d3-f08b-53ed-88ec-78f9962aecbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/9b6a2c5b3c9fdded8d615980df00228e2637515af0a23f62ae58b891bec3e3e7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377598} {:answer/id #uuid "10ad5c19-eb2d-58b4-a40a-07bf5e268d01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/c9adb78f087651508a43514756abbc45170766d6bb14a069053c4bf71e78e0a3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377599} {:answer/id #uuid "fafb19bb-f0f0-509b-8925-949bfdddb96b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/a1b02e904c1df760f47d623d3ff17538c284d7ef6125b786165113a383c79c3c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377600} {:answer/id #uuid "4707e5bb-ac4f-5c30-a615-39865fdd5334" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/16ff13c793d3c3f57eefee638fbc1ff83569ef6d084d9d053f8585f72c8812d7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377601} {:answer/id #uuid "7fee0fbf-dbb3-55f5-befc-a5d36eca3788" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/d42f10aac16e2c7405fbf158dacb1b46137cc03800f8660d0ff4cfe11c9edc63.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377602}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4707e5bb-ac4f-5c30-a615-39865fdd5334" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/16ff13c793d3c3f57eefee638fbc1ff83569ef6d084d9d053f8585f72c8812d7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377601} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9585d6a-5e2c-5083-80c8-10c8741648f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377597}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9854e016-8489-5582-ad5c-ffa3a5b6e5ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-181959" :question/problem "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S=N\\times Z$ in the Cartesian plane?" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS=N\\times Z\n$$\n\ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is a natural number and whose $y$-coordinate is an integer number.\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in N,\\,y\\in Z}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider ${S}_{1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any integer number.\n\n$$\n{S}_{1}={(1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in Z}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/179fdaa3921cb29f2d25ff97bce262fd13fb2ff591c12e97a33953e95967f6fc.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{2}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $2$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any natural number.\n\n$$\n{S}_{2}={(2,y)\\,:\\,y\\in Z}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{2}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/7399e42988df8a809013393d8873b6a6e5f60b64c0419eadd7bb3c334eb410d6.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets ${S}_{3},{S}_{4},{S}_{5},…$ similarly. Our Cartesian product $S$ is the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={S}_{1}\\cup {S}_{2}\\cup {S}_{3}\\cup … \\\\  & ={⋃}_{i\\in N}{S}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize $N\\times Z$ as the set of points in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/16ff13c793d3c3f57eefee638fbc1ff83569ef6d084d9d053f8585f72c8812d7.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307981 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2aee319-68d6-54f7-8023-b24d32610bc4" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching Cartesian Products of Special Sets in the Coordinate Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cac57808-fb29-58a9-9a51-a3760b4bd0d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/8de1a050eed07445dd4a80be2809e275419238f72e098693bfc16