9-884b-4ec060248a86" :question/math-academy-id "q-181966" :question/problem "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S=Z\\times Z$ in the Cartesian plane?" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS=Z\\times Z\n$$\n\ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is an integer number and whose $y$-coordinate is an integer number.\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in Z,\\,y\\in Z}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider ${S}_{-1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $-1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any integer number.\n\n$$\n{S}_{-1}={(-1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in Z}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{-1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/9673951b4e72ba503e740369462f9e528e43ffc083706ee558ade7ccd52702f5.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{0}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $0$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any integer number.\n\n$$\n{S}_{0}={(0,y)\\,:\\,y\\in Z}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{0}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/797b66a293eb241f841869abe97e324f4301907f71029acceefd68487bef1b4f.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets $…,{S}_{-3},{S}_{-2},{S}_{1},{S}_{2},{S}_{3},…$ similarly. Our Cartesian product $S$ is the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =…\\cup {S}_{-3}\\cup {S}_{-2}\\cup {S}_{-1}\\cup {S}_{0}\\cup {S}_{1}\\cup \\text{ }{S}_{2}\\cup {S}_{3}… \\\\  & ={⋃}_{i\\in Z}{S}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize $Z\\times Z$ as the set of points in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/de19fcf0f246b09d4bb957568e026b36de3f55c1a6c7065d79faacee794a8326.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377603 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2aee319-68d6-54f7-8023-b24d32610bc4" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching Cartesian Products of Special Sets in the Coordinate Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c68e871a-0a82-58bb-b642-5b68d643546d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/2df87b90d770a5f2c0ba40a55330409d1cd80866b7e34299eab6dcbf675c6a13.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377605} {:answer/id #uuid "78d5da05-0d63-5c07-bacb-f6a347d15b54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/5888cf137eea674995b4c9cc37285b4130df5de3a2a2fa63b1b5b195910526c1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377606} {:answer/id #uuid "e0d00a18-7602-5260-b18f-fbc3c13ece33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/2f28e6f8c8e6e9fa13b5285e25e5460d26548ffd9d71077d1c6073e6c4c743a1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377607} {:answer/id #uuid "49fb9617-3199-5b83-af26-fe562937296b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/463ae2387423a48dd557af8cfd9b01b6bc08b95f1b3c2f337c6b0c53d3731fa2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377608} {:answer/id #uuid "5c7d3cf6-9fe1-5fbd-bd15-f1bbe958cfe9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/f7e54dd973126e3179e79b1ba1a26f4a7c1c541ca764b14c4ccef73b4452509e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377609}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c7d3cf6-9fe1-5fbd-bd15-f1bbe958cfe9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/f7e54dd973126e3179e79b1ba1a26f4a7c1c541ca764b14c4ccef73b4452509e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377609} :answer-field/id #uuid "3df13d12-44e0-549e-861d-8aba8be0e35f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377604}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6bae802a-d591-53eb-b628-21b012d03cee" :question/math-academy-id "q-181968" :question/problem "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S={Z}^{-}\\times N$ in the Cartesian plane?" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS={Z}^{-}\\times N\n$$\n\ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is a negative integer and whose $y$-coordinate is a natural number.\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in {Z}^{-},\\,y\\in N}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider ${S}_{-1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $-1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any natural number.\n\n$$\n{S}_{-1}={(-1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in N}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{-1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/8e6816767cd2010fca1b8991ed9c5e0c42d3a6d6466d78025534bc8362a174b9.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{-2}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $-2$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any natural number.\n\n$$\n{S}_{-2}={(-2,y)\\,:\\,y\\in N}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{-2}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/4d0fe261e790520e6a8786e6f024d5b9cb7cd54c18242103a1f4b77f09653abc.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets $…,{S}_{-5},{S}_{-4},{S}_{-3}$ similarly. Our Cartesian product $S$ is the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =…{S}_{-4}\\cup {S}_{-3}\\cup {S}_{-2}\\cup {S}_{-1} \\\\  & ={⋃}_{i\\in {Z}^{-}}{S}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize ${Z}^{-}\\times N$ as the set of points in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/f7e54dd973126e3179e79b1ba1a26f4a7c1c541ca764b14c4ccef73b4452509e.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198734 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3a2b291-c6dd-58c8-a10f-d851511d7bfc" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching Cartesian Products in the Coordinate Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db693b49-2bec-59ba-91d8-3f26a5d1d9dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4387/Source/Images/q-181987-a-4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195461} {:answer/id #uuid "123dada4-b1fa-58e4-9523-a85c56398af1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4387/Source/Images/q-181987-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195462} {:answer/id #uuid "34a7d35a-8582-569d-a279-cf93880bbbc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4387/Source/Images/q-181987-a-2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195463} {:answer/id #uuid "39ef5d1e-89e9-50ad-b865-5cb8beed15b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4387/Source/Images/q-181987-a-5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195464} {:answer/id #uuid "611e76b4-a957-5ff1-8317-0bab4ca89c27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4387/Source/Images/q-181987-a-3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195465}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "123dada4-b1fa-58e4-9523-a85c56398af1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4387/Source/Images/q-181987-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195462} :answer-field/id #uuid "35fa6e74-a2da-5824-8c02-9458dddb39ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195460}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3bd0f51c-7480-5612-9de3-790f45702d19" :question/math-academy-id "q-181987" :question/problem "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S={1,2}\\times (-1,1)$ in the Cartesian plane?" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS={1,2}\\times (-1,1)\n$$\n\ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is either $1$ or $2$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $(-1,1).$\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in {1,2},\\,y\\in (-1,1)}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider ${S}_{1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $(-1,1).$\n\n$$\n{S}_{1}={(1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in (-1,1)}\n$$\n\nThis set is represented by the line segment shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/11522acdfeaad022847fedbcf6052424216587f99f2691e80ed96152f30818a4.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{2}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $2$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $(-1,1).$\n\n$$\n{S}_{2}={(2,y)\\,:\\,y\\in (-1,1)}.\n$$\n\nThis set is represented by the line segment shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/90b6a9c00d366e4a03c8c974476fdf06bc9fa73c9c26e9a0cd6ba9a4bda8e66d.png)\n\nOur Cartesian product is the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\nS={S}_{1}\\cup {S}_{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $S$ can be represented by the two line segments shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/bbf0d5ecc57bc30c7a3f4db7a1d30b27633e4586b2768e315715df72ce41333f.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377610 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3a2b291-c6dd-58c8-a10f-d851511d7bfc" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching Cartesian Products in the Coordinate Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "410c6fde-0f9c-562f-ad13-272cad98373f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/09b74d8a7baf8c22cab4ad3841e53c7286cd72dd7523916544ea370595b199a9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377612} {:answer/id #uuid "3c78f06c-f6d2-516b-9bfd-0376e1048cea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/70f7c02b3bca2b816858eaef766b5bbf3ced8d8abf101ef38ecda19b73781ee3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377613} {:answer/id #uuid "e1d27bae-db90-55d7-9e7c-0890fb3a01e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/aa3411e662566a609de5163269dbc1e7c0ad92f415b52936c5ef568d7058d2d2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377614} {:answer/id #uuid "3873d6f6-5e99-5129-afc6-d3a38b2c46ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/2eec72a5444533c1bb4f86836cf8479b76ae38d69c35f5f5eef5fd313a2365f0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377615} {:answer/id #uuid "7282a5a4-444b-5044-bdd7-c10dda9f7356" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/01d87db018bcbbf013d7f33519418849fe70e0d772870335482a7b07cfc89fd9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377616}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "410c6fde-0f9c-562f-ad13-272cad98373f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/09b74d8a7baf8c22cab4ad3841e53c7286cd72dd7523916544ea370595b199a9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377612} :answer-field/id #uuid "f807b770-75a0-567c-bfe2-245468d1f71b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377611}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "659e44f3-afb3-574d-a533-7467d2366762" :question/math-academy-id "q-184489" :question/problem "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S={-1,2}\\times {-3,1}$ in the Cartesian plane?" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nIn this case, computing ${-1,2}\\times {-3,1}$ gives the following set:\n\n$$\nS={(-1,-3),(-1,1),(2,-3),(2,1)}.\n$$\n\nOur visualization of this set is shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/09b74d8a7baf8c22cab4ad3841e53c7286cd72dd7523916544ea370595b199a9.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308016 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3a2b291-c6dd-58c8-a10f-d851511d7bfc" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching Cartesian Products in the Coordinate Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ed00671f-39a8-5ef5-acae-7a418bfae476" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/30f5c0611fe32d0bd14d838bcb45a02d468b7f7ef5e37a5c48ecd7648b306fbd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308018} {:answer/id #uuid "3bd104da-d32f-5629-8097-71eb5656acf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/2d72a5616d611fa8e437fbf5f689e0b1209c8b77b0c2fb92f72c6388d79faa60.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308019} {:answer/id #uuid "e1c43e22-76aa-5964-9101-227e8b59cc95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/d19ecceb011b54492d3ea9f0d262de1ba96e21c3423ed8b083d44551a6420db9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308020} {:answer/id #uuid "55bf2f9f-11c6-5e73-847b-42e36eb055f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/7c9ed47fedfe4a23ceb80847ac7a4805afecd1c9ff9028b4800b71e0870bef75.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308021} {:answer/id #uuid "d3632e7d-cf62-5316-9d9d-cdf9a2858da6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/d58cfce54cdf98d4ea8661ba91353ad5748b715d61d7f666db27a5cdef2d2431.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308022}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1c43e22-76aa-5964-9101-227e8b59cc95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/d19ecceb011b54492d3ea9f0d262de1ba96e21c3423ed8b083d44551a6420db9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308020} :answer-field/id #uuid "bcc71534-ffab-5f5b-9599-1e15722d52b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308017}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "475f1c7e-a5b8-58e4-b569-fdfc8a373029" :question/math-academy-id "q-184492" :question/problem "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S={-1,3}\\times [-2,1]$ in the Cartesian plane?" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS={-1,3}\\times [2,1]\n$$\n\ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is either $-1$ or $3$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[-2,1].$\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in {-1,3},\\,y\\in [-2,1]}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider ${S}_{1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $-1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[-2,1].$\n\n$$\n{S}_{1}={(-1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in [-2,1]}\n$$\n\nThis set is represented by the line segment shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/57cb74832a9766c60819bdcc0c88290c4a05556b673965973e161895de2f530a.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{2}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $3$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[-2,1].$\n\n$$\n{S}_{2}={(3,y)\\,:\\,y\\in [-2,1]}.\n$$\n\nThis set is represented by the line segment shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/9f3d0beca2ed9eb30eb6456b67d5f17002726e9b9de6e8ed3c79d72f43bcf3ae.png)\n\nOur Cartesian product is the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\nS={S}_{1}\\cup {S}_{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $S$ can be represented by the two line segments shown below.\n\n \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/d19ecceb011b54492d3ea9f0d262de1ba96e21c3423ed8b083d44551a6420db9.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308030 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6290e701-b4ea-5a86-ab7e-7371c5bdc11e" :knowledge-point/title "Visualizing Unions of Uncountable Families of Indexed Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0f10b47-a5b1-555f-9a65-f01bd18b2194" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/4d714297c6acc363f55a775fb3f6007145dec3aa415476d5c758f8c2def679ea.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308032} {:answer/id #uuid "72f5ef30-281f-569e-9fb0-36811ba46ad3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/86cd6fb9fd52708dd41257a7df95db869ce6638e0dde902e2f7847d09021b530.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308033} {:answer/id #uuid "545f030d-777e-5a51-af82-0d8f610fe650" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/204c9a31031534ac70a599322c3f77a077440300fde2364476643c88406e34cd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308034} {:answer/id #uuid "53d1f208-7243-51f1-80f1-4a9ab9a8303a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/748c2e450098f81b24d22a34ce375b1ea365c4879a33bfb2a539065e9e800e15.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308035} {:answer/id #uuid "70a343ac-096d-5398-9893-ad1f34b1890c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/3a713f172fca69aa423ae4664f2a6e31eb4315b666f17b9ea4a7c1ffbc8322be.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308036}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "545f030d-777e-5a51-af82-0d8f610fe650" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/204c9a31031534ac70a599322c3f77a077440300fde2364476643c88406e34cd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308034} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d53f14a-8347-5fc1-a7fb-c57ae444d049" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308031}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "05268f20-60a4-56fb-b13c-c24e63cbe9c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-184505" :question/problem "Let $I=[0,1]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}={i}\\times [0,{i}^{3}].$ Which of the following represents ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the union of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *at least one* set:\n\n$$\n{⋃}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}={x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for at least one}\\,\\,k\\in I}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=0.5,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{0.5}={0.5}\\times [0,0.125]={(0.5,k)\\,:\\,k\\in [0,0.125]}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a vertical line segment from the point $(0.5,0)$ to the point $(0.5,0.125)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/3a1bb7a7c54718e39df80092243a119ec8b5a6e77ec4f9d58ffb074e50191d04.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}={i}\\times [0,{i}^{3}]={(i,k)\\,:\\,k\\in [0,{i}^{3}]}\n$$\n\nis the vertical line segment from the point $(i,0)$ to the point $(i,{i}^{3}).$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/472b6456019a5c89e585f80aa4a0dfd44f3b87c0624c10ea7b52f32e958a8c4e.png)\n\nAs $i$ runs over $I,$ these line segments sweep the following figure that represents the union ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/204c9a31031534ac70a599322c3f77a077440300fde2364476643c88406e34cd.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377617 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uui